{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states during the 12th and 13th centuries. He was a powerful and influential ruler known for his military prowess political acumen and efforts to consolidate and strengthen the County of Tripoli. Born around 1134 Raymond inherited the County at a young age upon the death of his father Raymond II. His early reign was marked by internal challenges and external threats primarily from Muslim forces.\n\n*Early Reign and Consolidation*\n\nRaymond III faced immediate opposition from rival factions within the County and from neighboring Muslim rulers. He skillfully navigated these challenges through a combination of diplomacy military campaigns and strategic alliances. He focused on rebuilding the County's fortifications strengthening its economy and fostering loyalty among his vassals. A key aspect of his early reign involved dealing with the Seljuk Turks who posed a constant danger to Tripoli's borders. Raymond engaged in numerous military expeditions against them often achieving tactical victories that helped to stabilize the region. He also worked to maintain good relations with other Crusader states particularly the Kingdom of Jerusalem recognizing the importance of collective defense.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant expansion of Tripoli's territory and influence. He extended control over several coastal towns and fortified positions increasing the County's economic resources and military strength. He invested heavily in infrastructure including the construction and repair of castles and roads improving trade routes and facilitating communication. His policies aimed at creating a prosperous and secure environment for his subjects attracting merchants artisans and settlers to the region.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Conflicts*\n\nThroughout his reign Raymond III was actively involved in military campaigns against Muslim forces. He engaged in numerous battles against the Ayyubid dynasty a powerful Muslim dynasty that rose to prominence in the Levant. These conflicts often involved sieges raids and open field battles. Raymond demonstrated considerable military skill and leadership consistently outmaneuvering his opponents and inflicting heavy losses. He played a crucial role in defending Tripoli from Ayyubid incursions preserving its independence and preventing its annexation. His military campaigns were not solely defensive; he also launched offensive operations aimed at expanding Tripoli's territory and disrupting Muslim trade routes.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a skilled diplomat adept at forging alliances and maintaining peaceful relations with various powers. He understood the importance of diplomacy in preserving Tripoli's security and advancing its interests. He cultivated relationships with the Papacy the Holy Roman Empire and other Crusader states seeking support and assistance when needed. He also engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to establish truces and maintain a degree of stability. His diplomatic efforts were often instrumental in preventing large-scale conflicts and securing favorable outcomes for Tripoli. Raymond's reign represents a golden age for the County of Tripoli a period of relative peace prosperity and stability in a turbulent region. He left a lasting legacy as a capable ruler a skilled military leader and a shrewd diplomat who helped to shape the history of the Crusader states. He died in 1194 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0017849955474957824}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁reigned", "logprob": -1.6283552646636963}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.05146126